    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The ultimate goal of robust ASR systems is to learn well the relationship between noisy speech and the word predictions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">it is quite difcult for non-experts to use/develop ASR systems for new a</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The de facto standard metric to evaluate the performance of ASR systems is Word Error Rate (WER)</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">it is quite difcult for non-experts to use/develop ASR systems</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ASR systems are generally not robust to mismatched training and testing conditions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">speech recognition systems in low-resource languages</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The automatic speech recognition (ASR) system combines 4 diverse</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The automatic speech recognition (ASR) system</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RNNLM based ASR system</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">increase the robustness of seq-to-seq models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Sequence-to-sequence models have become popular in the automatic speech recognition (ASR) community [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ], as they allow for one neural network to jointly learn an acoutic, pronunciation and language model</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ASR systems for noise robustness</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">ASR systems under deployment</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Consider a typical use-case for ASR systems under deployment.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Most frequent deletion and insertion errors for humans and ASR system</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The de facto standard metric to evaluate the performance of ASR systems is Word Error Rate (WER) or Word Accuracy Rate (WAR).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">subsume the acoustic, pronunciation and language model components of a traditional automatic speech recognition (ASR) system into a single neural network.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">large RNNLM based ASR system</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the output of existing ASR systems.</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
