    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">1910.06711_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">1807.07281_rag::chunk_002</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2110.12612_rag::chunk_002</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2106.02297_rag::chunk_000</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Vocoders generate waveform from either linguistic features or acoustic features</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A conditional waveform generation module (a.k.a. vocoder) adds the phase information and synthesizes a detailed waveform.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the WORLD vocoder (MORISE et al., 2016) introduces an in</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">they actually depend on a traditional vocoder ( Morise et al. , 2016 ), the Grifn-Lim algorithm ( Grifn and Lim , 1984 ), or a separately trained neural vocoder</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">conditional waveform generation module (a.k.a. vocoder) adds the phase information and synthesizes a detailed waveform</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">vocoder only need to recover phase information given ground-truth spectrogram</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">recent works on neural vocoder have improved the quality of synthesized audio</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
